    "model": "Desai and Popova both invite the audience to reconsider sleeplessness, but where Desai treats it comically as a sign of a young man's alienation from those around him, Popova elevates it into a serious existential condition. Desai's onomatopoeia, \"Rrrrr. Rrrr. Phurt Wheeeeee.\", and the hyperbole of a fan \"making as much noise as a gale\" render the family's snoring absurd, while the adjective in \"each hostile inhalation\" exposes Sampath's self-absorbed perspective, so we laugh at his indignation even as we sense his loneliness: \"Even his mother, whom he loved most of all, had forgotten him in sleep.\" Popova, by contrast, writes in an authoritative essayistic voice, and her indictment of \"the cult of productivity gone past the sacrifice of presence\" invites readers to see a cultural self-betrayal rather than a private grievance. Both texts link wakefulness to estrangement: Sampath's sleeping family, \"their hips rising like mountains far too high to climb\", become distant giants, much as Pessoa, quoted by Popova, goes \"to life's bed wide awake, unaccompanied and without peace\". Yet whereas Desai leaves Sampath weighed down \"like a grinding stone\", Popova finds in insomnia \"a metaphysical knowledge of the mystery of things\", inviting the audience to view restlessness not only as suffering but as a source of insight.",
