    "criteria": "7 – Provides a perceptive, sustained comparison of how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with precise evidence from both texts integrated throughout\n6 – Provides an effective comparison of how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with relevant evidence from both texts\n5 – Provides a sound comparison of how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with some evidence from both texts\n4 – Compares how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with some evidence from both texts\n3 – Explains how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with limited comparison or evidence\n2 – Describes aspects of how the composers of Text 1 and Text 2 use their forms to represent superficiality as a human quality, with limited supporting evidence\n1 – Provides some relevant information about the texts",
