    "model": "The cover and Bukowski’s poem both expose superficiality as a performance that substitutes display for genuine engagement, but the cover makes that performance collectively visible while the poem adopts a voice of hostile refusal. The magazine cover’s conventional masthead, “THE NEW YORKER”, frames a street as a fashion spread: posed figures in bright pinks, yellows and reds stand out against the cool grey-blue street, while crouching photographers and phones turn everyday movement into content. Its converging street and dozens of small figures make this a social habit rather than one person’s vanity; the cover’s polished, painterly design offers an attractive surface that mirrors the attractiveness being performed. Bukowski’s free-verse form works differently. The lower-case lines and enjambment in “the final curtain on one of the longest running / musicals ever” refuse the ceremonial shape of a polished tribute, while the flat catalogue “flowers, cheers, tears” compresses public emotion into clichés. The poem’s single, irregular stanza moves from the reported spectacle to the speaker’s judgement, and the repeated “thunderous / accolade” is ironic: applause is loud but empty. Sound also sharpens the rejection, with the blunt consonants of “sickened me” and the dismissive direct address “trust me on this” replacing communal celebration with abrasive self-assertion. Yet the speaker’s wish that others’ entertainment “will / keep them from my door” reveals his own self-absorption. Both forms therefore make superficiality visible through a gap between surface and substance: the cover’s colourful, camera-filled design seduces the eye, whereas the poem’s lineation, stanza movement and abrasive sounds strip away the glamour.",
