    "model": "Cave and Ottaway both present water as answering complex desires for relief, remembrance and renewal, but Cave seeks ecstatic escape while Ottaway seeks continuing courage. Cave’s letter turns wild swimming into a ‘radical rearrangement’ of his relationship with the world; the hyperbole ‘catastrophic outrage to the nervous system’ makes immersion both painful and desired. His startling claim that it is ‘simply impossible to grieve in icy water’ does not deny bereavement but reveals a wish for temporary release, while the allusion to Deacon’s ‘giggling idiot’ and the paradox of ‘internal ecstatic screaming’ capture joy emerging from bodily shock. The closing list ‘unsupervised, illicit, subversive’ makes swimming a rebellious ‘antidote to despair’, so happiness is intense yet deliberately sought. Ottaway’s poem gives water a longer emotional history. The metaphor ‘saltwater brain’ fuses memory, body and place, and the contrast between ‘turbid water’ and ‘tender water’ holds childbirth’s fear and care together. Personification makes water a confidant that ‘has heard my sorrows’ and ‘never leaves me’, satisfying a desire for constancy. Her final request for ‘clean forgetfulness that engenders courage’ is paradoxical like Cave’s revelation, but it asks not to obliterate feeling so much as to be strengthened by release. Both writers therefore show immersion as more than recreation: it answers grief, preserves identity and creates renewal. Cave dramatises an urgent escape from despair; Ottaway offers a quieter, lifelong companionship with nature.",
