    "model": "Keegan and Vuong both explore identity as shaped by forces beyond the individual, but Keegan presents a self defined by the shared rhythms of community and work, while Vuong presents a self forged against the judgement of others. Keegan's narration moves from the collective to the individual: the list of \"shop-keepers and tradesmen, men and women in the post office and the dole queue\" absorbs people into one weather-beaten town before Furlong is introduced by his trade as \"the coal and timber merchant\", suggesting identity is conferred by role. Faith structures this world, and the violent metaphor of cold that would \"cut the knees off those who still knelt to say the rosary\" hints at a tradition under strain. Vuong, by contrast, writes in the first person about language as the force that shapes him. The simile of reading as \"standing in quicksand, your loved ones corralled at its safe edges\" shows family loyalty and literacy pulling against each other, and the teacher examining his poem \"the way one might examine counterfeit money\" reveals how institutions deny an outsider's identity. Whereas Keegan's characters accept their place, Vuong reclaims his: the paradox \"I have plagiarized my life to give you the best of me\" turns an accusation into self-definition. Both writers therefore show identity as formed by the communities around us, though only Vuong resists those forces.",
